{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-154002",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-2.$ To isolate $z,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-2.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2z\\,\\, & <\\,\\,7 \\\\ \\frac{-2z}{-2}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,\\frac{7}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2z}{-2}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2z}{-2} & >-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}z}{\\cancel{-2}} & >-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & >-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z>-\\frac{7}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
